  "atoms": [
    {
      "id": "a0",
      "pos": 1,
      "text": "Cognitivism holds that moral judgments express beliefs capable of being true or false.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a1",
      "pos": 2,
      "text": "Non-cognitivism holds that moral judgments express desires, emotions, or other non-belief attitudes.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a2",
      "pos": 3,
      "text": "Motivational internalism, the thesis that sincere moral judgment entails some motivation to act, is standardly cited in support of non-cognitivism.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a3",
      "pos": 4,
      "text": "Hume argued in the Treatise that moral distinctions are derived from reason alone.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a4",
      "pos": 5,
      "text": "A.J. Ayer defended emotivism about moral language in Language, Truth and Logic.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a5",
      "pos": 6,
      "text": "C.L. Stevenson argued in Ethics and Language that moral terms carry emotive meaning alongside descriptive meaning.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a6",
      "pos": 7,
      "text": "P.T. Geach pressed the embedding problem against non-cognitivist accounts of moral vocabulary.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7",
      "pos": 8,
      "text": "Simon Blackburn developed quasi-realism to account for the realist-seeming surface of moral discourse.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a8",
      "pos": 9,
      "text": "The Frege\u2013Geach problem concerns the constant meaning of moral terms in unasserted contexts such as the antecedents of conditionals.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a9",
      "pos": 10,
      "text": "Blackburn's quasi-realism concedes that moral judgments describe mind-independent moral properties.",
      "factual": false,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a10",
      "pos": 11,
      "text": "J.L. Mackie's argument from queerness claims that objective moral values would be metaphysically unlike anything else in the universe.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a11",
      "pos": 12,
      "text": "Mackie's argument from relativity appeals to the variation of moral codes across human societies.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a12",
      "pos": 13,
      "text": "Mackie classified himself as a defender of objective moral values.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    },
    {
      "id": "a13",
      "pos": 14,
      "text": "Mackie denied that objective moral values exist.",
      "factual": true,
      "stance": "asserted"
    }
